# An EXACT non-generic overload must win over a generic of the same name.
#
# stage1 answered 2 here where stage0 answers 1 — a SILENT WRONG ANSWER: no decline, no
# link error, just a call bound to the wrong body. It survived a green 148-check gate and a
# byte-identical gen3 fixpoint because the compiler's own source has no overload shaped
# like this, and no corpus program did either.
#
# TWO defects, each hiding the other:
#   * the declare/emit passes skipped a declaration whose NAME matched a generic, so the
#     non-generic overload was never emitted at all (`nm`: only `_f__i64`, no `_f`);
#   * call resolution tried generics before non-generic overloads.
# Fixing either alone changes nothing, which is what made this take three attempts to
# diagnose. The same defect is why the std's `print` could not be called.
def f(x: i64) -> i64:
    return 1

def f[T](x: T) -> i64:
    return 2

def main() -> i64:
    typed: i64 = 5
    # Both spellings: a bare literal (which types as Unmodeled and adopts its context) and
    # an already-typed local. Both must select the non-generic overload.
    return f(5) + f(typed)
